---
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
---

<SiteLayout
	description="Backbeat makes getting and sharing rhythm game content easy."
	title="Backbeat"
>
	<main class="home">
		<section class="intro">
			<h1>
				<span>Backbeat</span>
			</h1>
			<p class="intro-definition">
				Backbeat is a tool that makes getting rhythm game charts, difficulty tables, packs
				and courses <b>easy</b>.
			</p>
			<div class="intro-actions">
				<a class="download" href="/get-started">Get Started</a>
				<a class="transparent-button" href="/docs/">Developer Docs</a>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="what-section" id="what-is-backbeat">
			<header class="section-heading">
				<h2>What is Backbeat?</h2>
				<p>
					You can think of Backbeat as a special "Songs/" folder on your computer, that
					all games can read from.
					<br>
					Backbeat works <b>for any rhythm game</b>. It's not locked down to any specific
					game.
					<br>
					Get the app, download some content, and download a rhythm game with support for
					Backbeat!
				</p>
			</header>
		</section>

		<section class="why-section" id="why">
			<header class="section-heading">
				<h2>Why Backbeat?</h2>
			</header>
			<dl class="why-points">
				<div>
					<dt>It doesn't suck</dt>
					<dd>
						There's no "extract the zip in korean locale", "put this folder at exactly
						this position" or "ensure the stars are aligned in your bms folder".
						<br>
						<br>
						No more. You ask for a chart, a pack, a table, a dan course - and you get
						it.
					</dd>
				</div>
				<div>
					<dt>It's faster and lighter</dt>
					<dd>
						Backbeat doesn't download duplicates of files. This saves storage space
						{" "}<i>and</i>{" "}
						download times!
						<br>
						<br>
						You can go from an empty store to a full BMS install of every table in
						{" "}<i>just one hour</i>, and downloading new charts doesn't require you to
						re-download files you already have.
					</dd>
				</div>
				<div>
					<dt>It's not a centralised server</dt>
					<dd>
						Anyone can host a backbeat server. It's trivial. It's designed in such a way
						that you can add servers <i>you don't trust</i>, and can still be sure that
						you got the right content.
						<br>
						<br>
						Content on Backbeat doesn't go missing, it doesn't get shutterstock'd, and
						it can't bitrot over the years.
					</dd>
				</div>
				<div>
					<dt>It's game-agnostic</dt>
					<dd>
						Backbeat has no game-specific logic. It works for every game ever made, and
						every game that ever will be made.
					</dd>
				</div>
				<div>
					<dt>It has cool features</dt>
					<dd>
						Games that integrate backbeat get native difficulty tables, which are a fun
						way of assigning difficulties to chart without the woes of a difficulty
						calculator.
						<br>
						<br>
						You also get packs and courses!
					</dd>
				</div>
				<div>
					<dt>It's easy to integrate</dt>
					<dd>
						It's a well documented SDK with no moving parts. There's no service to run
						on your computer, no additional config. You launch a game with backbeat
						support and the user doesn't even need to know about it.
						<br>
						<br>
						You can just think of it as a managed <code>Songs/</code> folder on
						steroids.
					</dd>
				</div>
			</dl>
		</section>

		<section class="get-started-section">
			<a class="get-started-banner" href="/get-started">
				<span class="get-started-banner-copy">
					<strong>Get started with Backbeat</strong>
					<span>Download the app, download some content, and do some gaming.</span>
				</span>
				<span aria-hidden="true" class="get-started-banner-arrow">↗</span>
			</a>
		</section>
	</main>
</SiteLayout>
